Z||000000||spike||03-08-2000||08:45 AM||||I am trying to decide on a fair price for my Custom made Sub Box. As I have mentioned the material cost about 80-100 CDN. The time to complete 1 box is about 4-6 HRS. All sides of the box are angled which makes it harder to build. The box will come complete less Subs. It will be able to hold 2 10 inchers. I have decided to only offer this version and not the 2 by 12's. The face plate of the box will be covered with matching vynle. Either in Gray, Beige or Black the choice is yours. The most expensive part to the box is the plexiglass windows. Plexiglass is guite expensive, and I need 2 pieces per box. I which I had pictures but sorry I don't. You will loose about 1-1.5 inch's of seat travel and not more. I would realy like to have your opinions on this matter. I have never seen or heard of a setup like mine, have you? There is nothing needed to hold the box in place it is a custom fit which molds to the interior contours of the Fiero. Trust me the box does look like it belongs there. Thanks for your help. If anyone is looking for a nice system with plenty of BASS well this box is for you. I love big bass that is why I built this box for my Fiero. The first box took days to build. I am very fussy about the way things look and this box looks<BR>perfect in the fiero. I along with many friends could not believe how good it looked.<BR>It looked like it came new that way. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||03-08-2000||11:01 AM||||I'm looking into doin one sub box but to be placed all the way to the front of the passenger floor area in an angle way under the dash. My car is an 88 Formula with A/C. I'm looking into the idea of doing some odd fiberglass shape that will go up to the original sub area (elbow shape) to take those open cubic inches so it takes less space under tha dash area. Currently I have a cube box (10" sub) which doesn't take too much space but is a little unconfortable and odd looking. If I am successfull I will present pictures here. I expect to finish it by June.||||reg||1
Z||000002||olympic||03-08-2000||04:38 PM||||     A general rule for figuring out a retail price is to take the cost of the product, including your labour costs(say $10/hour), and double it.  Since you are not operating a store and your over head is low, you may be able to sell for less, say, cost plus 50%.||||reg||3
